# Copy to .env. main.py, main_temporal.py and mock_llm.py all load it
# automatically.

# --- Required ---
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...

# --- Which endpoint the app talks to ---
# Default: the local mock server (start it with `uv run mock_llm.py`)
OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/v1
# Live OpenAI API — comment out the line above and uncomment this one
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1

# --- Mock server ---
DELAY_SECONDS=2
# RECORDINGS=recordings/gpt-4o-mini
# PORT=8000

# ===========================================================================
# Failure scenarios — one per workshop challenge
# ===========================================================================
# Scenario N is the config for challenge N. There are eight of each and the
# numbers line up on purpose, so "challenge 6" and "scenario 6" always mean
# the same thing.
#
#   1-4  run against main.py           (plain Python, the OpenAI SDK retries)
#   5-8  run against main_temporal.py  (a Temporal Workflow, Temporal retries)
#
# Each pair 1:5, 2:6, 3:7, 4:8 is the same failure told twice — once without
# durable execution and once with. Where a pair's config is identical, the
# scenario is still written out in full rather than cross-referenced, so you
# can enable a challenge without reading any other block.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How the knobs work
#
# ANALYSIS_STATUS / RESPONSE_STATUS take one HTTP status per attempt,
# comma-separated, then cycle. Restart the mock server after changing them.
#
# "requests" = how many the mock serves for one run. The app only ever makes
# two calls; anything above that is a retry.
#
#   main.py (1-4)          the OpenAI SDK retries in-process. It retries twice
#                          by default for 429 and 5xx, never for 4xx.
#                          MAX_RETRIES is the app-side budget.
#   main_temporal.py (5-8) SDK retries are OFF (max_retries=0). The activity's
#                          RetryPolicy in workflow.py is the only retry
#                          mechanism, so MAX_RETRIES does nothing here.
#
# Keep sequences to 3 entries or fewer for 1-4 — that is the SDK's whole
# budget (1 try + 2 retries). Longer sequences make consecutive runs differ.
#
# To enable a scenario: uncomment its block and comment out every other one,
# including any STICKY_STATUS, MAX_RETRIES or FALLBACK_PAUSE_SECONDS it sets.
# Then restart the mock server.
# ===========================================================================


# --- 1-4: main.py -------------------------------------------------------

# 1. Challenge 01 — The Pipeline Works.                                [ACTIVE]
#    Healthy. 2 requests, succeeds. Nothing fails; this is the shape.
ANALYSIS_STATUS=200
RESPONSE_STATUS=200

# 2. Challenge 02 — Retries Doing Their Job.
#    Call 2 flaky, recovers. 4 requests, succeeds.
#    Retries save the run — and the code still shows only one call.
# ANALYSIS_STATUS=200
# RESPONSE_STATUS=500,500,200

# 3. Challenge 03 — Losing Your Place.
#    A 504 that is retried but never recovers, so the run always fails. The
#    only thing that differs is whether the retry budget was honoured. Ctrl+C
#    the app once `retry` reaches 4 or so, then run it again: in the mock log
#    `retry` restarts at 0 while `seen` keeps climbing. The app lost its count;
#    the upstream did not.
#    MAX_RETRIES is an APP setting (the OpenAI client's budget). The mock never
#    reads it — it only ever sees the requests that budget produces. Raised to
#    10 here so the retry sequence is long enough to interrupt.
#    DELAY_SECONDS=0 because the SDK's own backoff already paces the run.
# ANALYSIS_STATUS=200
# RESPONSE_STATUS=504
# MAX_RETRIES=10
# DELAY_SECONDS=0

# 4. Challenge 04 — Retries Spent On A Wall.
#    Quota exhausted part-way through, and a decision that does not survive.
#    Call 1 succeeds and is paid for; Call 2 hits a 429 carrying
#    code=insufficient_quota, which STICKY_STATUS latches for that model — so
#    gpt-4o-mini returns it for everything from then on, across restarts, while
#    claude-opus-5 keeps working. A 429 says "transient" but insufficient_quota
#    is terminal, so the SDK's 3 attempts are wasted before the app sees it.
#
#    The app then handles it correctly on its own: it falls back to Anthropic and
#    finishes the ticket. Run it and let it finish to see that.
#
#    The point is what a crash costs in the FALLBACK_PAUSE_SECONDS window between
#    deciding and acting. Kill the app there, then run it again:
#      run 1: analysis 200, 3x 429, DECISION  -> kill it during the pause
#      run 2: 3x 429 on the analysis it already had, then redoes it on Anthropic
#    5 calls after the crash, 1 of them work that had not already been done.
#    Restart the mock server to clear the latch.
# ANALYSIS_STATUS=200
# RESPONSE_STATUS=429
# STICKY_STATUS=429
# FALLBACK_PAUSE_SECONDS=10


# --- 5-8: main_temporal.py (needs `uv run worker.py` running) -----------

# 5. Challenge 05 — The Pipeline Works, Durably.
#    Healthy. 2 requests, succeeds.
#    Identical config to scenario 1, deliberately: the same happy path, run by
#    a Workflow instead of a script. Written out in full so 5-8 stand alone.
# ANALYSIS_STATUS=200
# RESPONSE_STATUS=200

# 6. Challenge 06 — Retries Doing Their Job, Durably.
#    Call 2 flaky, recovers. 4 requests, succeeds.
#    Identical config to scenario 2. What changes is where the retry lives:
#    the RetryPolicy in workflow.py, not the OpenAI client. In the mock log
#    `retry` now stays 0 and `seen` does the counting — watch `seen=`.
# ANALYSIS_STATUS=200
# RESPONSE_STATUS=500,500,200

# 7. Challenge 07 — Losing Your Place, Durably.
#    Scenario 3's 504, answered. Kill the worker (Ctrl+C in its terminal) part
#    way through the retry sequence and restart it: the attempt count is held
#    by the server, so it resumes where it stopped instead of starting over.
#    NOT identical to scenario 3 — there is no MAX_RETRIES here. The SDK's
#    retries are off, so the budget is maximum_attempts=8 in workflow.py.
#    That is the whole difference: 8 requests here against scenario 3's 16.
#    DELAY_SECONDS=0 because Temporal's backoff paces the run.
# ANALYSIS_STATUS=200
# RESPONSE_STATUS=504
# DELAY_SECONDS=0

# 8. Challenge 08 — Retries Spent On A Wall, Durably.
#    Scenario 4's wall, answered. Same 429, same insufficient_quota latch. The
#    activity classifies it and raises a non-retryable error, so the budget is
#    not burned; the Workflow records the decision to switch provider, waits,
#    then falls back. Kill the worker during that wait and restart it — the
#    analysis is not redone and the decision is still there.
#    NOT identical to scenario 4 — there is no FALLBACK_PAUSE_SECONDS here.
#    Workflow code is replayed, so the pause is a timedelta literal in
#    workflow.py rather than something read from the environment.
# ANALYSIS_STATUS=200
# RESPONSE_STATUS=429
# STICKY_STATUS=429


# --- Optional ---
# MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# MAX_RETRIES=2               # app-side retry budget for main.py; 0 disables
#                             # retrying entirely. Ignored by main_temporal.py.
# FALLBACK_PAUSE_SECONDS=10   # app-side wait in main.py before failing over to
#                             # a second provider. Scenario 4 asks you to kill the
#                             # app in it, so raise it on a projector. Default
#                             # matches workflow.py's FALLBACK_PAUSE.

# Only needed if you re-add the Claude recorder script
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
